\subsection*{(c) PBE via backward induction (10 marks)}

We use backward induction: start from the University's decision node (after observing $N$), then work backwards to Peter's choice of $N$.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 1 — University's decision (given beliefs $\mu(H|N)$).}

Uni observes $N$ and decides Accept (A) or Reject (R).

\begin{itemize}
\item Accept payoff: $\mu(H|N)\cdot 1 + (1-\mu(H|N))\cdot(-1) = 2\mu(H|N)-1$.
\item Reject payoff: $0$.
\end{itemize}

Uni accepts iff $2\mu(H|N)-1 \geq 0$, i.e.:
\[
\boxed{\text{Uni accepts } \Leftrightarrow\ \mu(H|N) \geq 0.5.}
\]

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 2 — Peter's best response (given Uni's strategy).}

Peter knows his type and anticipates Uni's response. For each $N$, Peter compares acceptance versus rejection.

\textbf{Type H:} Acceptance payoff $= 5-2N$, Rejection payoff $= 0$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
$N$ & Accept & Reject & Best response \\ \hline
$0$ & $5$ & $0$ & Accept \\
$1$ & $3$ & $0$ & Accept \\
$2$ & $1$ & $0$ & Accept \\
$N\geq 3$ & $\leq -1$ & $0$ & Reject
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Type L:} Acceptance payoff $= 5-3N$, Rejection payoff $= 0$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
$N$ & Accept & Reject & Best response \\ \hline
$0$ & $5$ & $0$ & Accept \\
$1$ & $2$ & $0$ & Accept \\
$2$ & $-1$ & $0$ & Reject \\
$N\geq 3$ & $\leq -4$ & $0$ & Reject
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 3 — Sequential rationality of beliefs.}

Uni cannot observe Peter's type, only $N$. Both types $H$ and $L$ prefer $N=0$ (acceptance gives $5>0$, strictly better than any other $N$). Hence at any PBE, Peter plays $N=0$ with both types.

Thus Uni's on-path belief at $N=0$ equals the prior:
\[
\mu(H|0) = 0.2 < 0.5.
\]

Uni rejects at $N=0$ since accepting gives expected payoff $2(0.2)-1=-0.6<0$.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 4 — Off-path beliefs (sequential rationality requires specifying them).}

By the intuitive criterion, if Peter deviates to $N=1$ or $N=2$:

\begin{itemize}
\item At $N=1$: Only Type H deviates (Type L gets $5>2$ at equilibrium vs $2>0$ from acceptance). Hence $\mu(H|1)=1$. Uni accepts (expected payoff $2(1)-1=1>0$).
\item At $N=2$: Only Type H deviates (Type L gets $5>-1$ at equilibrium vs $-1<0$ from acceptance). Hence $\mu(H|2)=1$. Uni accepts (expected payoff $1>0$).
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 5 — Verify equilibrium.}

At $N=0$: Both types accept and get $5>0$ (rejection). Uni rejects (expected payoff $-0.6<0$). No profitable deviations for either player.

\bigskip
\[
\boxed{\text{PBE: } s_P^*(H)=s_P^*(L)=N=0;\quad s_U^*(0)=R,\ s_U^*(1)=A,\ s_U^*(2)=A.}
\]

\textbf{Can Uni learn?} No. Both types play the same signal $N=0$ in equilibrium, so observing $N=0$ gives no information about Peter's type. Uni cannot learn Peter's type from his strategy.

